From 2ab86d03e146eee1f17b61a759dd6e6a94160573 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:16:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): spend budget no longer counts owner-refusal runs
 that ran no turn

A card whose profile owner refused its claim fifteen times was held by the
spend budget as if it had spent fifteen runs (t_eeeeee0000000023: 8 real runs
plus 7 refusals, 6 owner_unavailable and 1 crashed rc 69, hit the 15-run limit).
Refused claims run no turn and spend nothing.

kanban_spend_budget.spend() now leaves out closed runs whose outcome is
owner_unavailable / owner_down, and crashed runs with exit_code 69 (the
owner-unreachable exit) and no usage receipt. Spend.excluded counts them and
`spend-budget` shows the count. A crashed run that reported usage, or any other
exit code, still counts. The cache-read token limit is unchanged.

Five whys:
1. Why was the card held? Its spend window counted 15 closed runs.
2. Why did refusals count? spend() selects task_runs by ended_at/duration only;
   a refusal that lasted MIN_RUN_SECONDS passes that filter.
3. Why wasn't that fixed with the failure-budget fix? t_eeeeee0000000005 and
   t_eeeeee0000000008 changed the dispatch failure counter only; the spend
   counter reads task_runs with its own filter.
4. Why did nothing catch it? No test tied the two budgets to one shared
   definition of "a turn ran".
5. Fix: the spend counter excludes no-turn owner refusals, with tests for the
   mixed window, the real-runs control, a crashed-with-usage control, the
   cache-read limit and the readback.

RED/GREEN: against the old source the 5 new tests in
tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_spend_budget.py fail (including the 8 real + 7
refusal window being held); with the fix the 8 test files importing
kanban_spend_budget pass (194 tests).
